WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.996
Welcome to Module 5, the last
module of Defender X D R

2
00:00:03.996 --> 00:00:09.163
Engineering and Operations:
automation, operations and evidence.

3
00:00:09.163 --> 00:00:12.100
Automation is where speed and outage meet.

4
00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:15.754
The same setting that contains a
compromised laptop in minutes can

5
00:00:15.754 --> 00:00:19.538
lock out the account the security
operations center needs to respond.

6
00:00:19.538 --> 00:00:24.071
In this module you set Defender's automation
to the program's response ladder,

7
00:00:24.071 --> 00:00:27.400
and exclude what must never
be contained automatically.

8
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:31.486
Then you run the platform: the
cadence, the monthly evidence run,

9
00:00:31.486 --> 00:00:35.100
the synthetic events, and the
metrics an assessor reads.

10
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:40.337
The module covers Steps 9 and 10 of the
Defender X D R implementation guide.

11
00:00:40.337 --> 00:00:44.840
It takes about forty minutes, plus
Lab 2 in your own test tenant.

12
00:00:48.590 --> 00:00:50.590
Why does automation need a boundary?

13
00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:52.253
Because it cuts both ways.

14
00:00:52.253 --> 00:00:57.219
On the speed side: Microsoft Learn recommends
the full remediation level for device

15
00:00:57.219 --> 00:01:01.973
groups, so threats on workstations are
remediated automatically, and Learn says

16
00:01:01.973 --> 00:01:06.514
every automatic attack disruption action
can be undone by the security team.

17
00:01:06.514 --> 00:01:10.590
On the outage side: a contained
server is an outage, and a contained

18
00:01:10.590 --> 00:01:14.815
emergency access account is a lockout,
in the middle of the incident.

19
00:01:14.815 --> 00:01:16.890
The guide names both as dangers.

20
00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:20.480
And Learn itself cautions that
exclusions reduce protection,

21
00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:22.690
so you can't exclude your way out either.

22
00:01:22.690 --> 00:01:25.288
The Security Operations
and Telemetry book's

23
00:01:25.288 --> 00:01:28.365
response ladder decides what
may act without a person.

24
00:01:28.365 --> 00:01:33.271
This module sets Defender's own automation
to match it, with two settings: each

25
00:01:33.271 --> 00:01:35.417
device group's remediation level,

26
00:01:35.417 --> 00:01:39.140
and the exclusions for
automatic attack disruption.

27
00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.803
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

28
00:01:45.803 --> 00:01:47.772
Name the five automation levels,

29
00:01:47.772 --> 00:01:51.127
and set each device group's
level to the response ladder.

30
00:01:51.127 --> 00:01:55.987
Record attack disruption exclusions for
emergency access and service accounts,

31
00:01:55.987 --> 00:01:58.489
with the authorizing official's approval.

32
00:01:58.489 --> 00:02:02.378
Run the monthly evidence run, and
check its hashes a month later.

33
00:02:02.378 --> 00:02:06.104
And use the synthetic events,
the metrics and the evidence

34
00:02:06.104 --> 00:02:10.141
catalog to show that the controls
work, not just that they exist.

35
00:02:10.141 --> 00:02:11.902
You need Modules 1 to 4.

36
00:02:11.902 --> 00:02:16.000
Skim the Security Operations
and Telemetry book's Chapter 11,

37
00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.777
for the response ladder, and
Chapter 12, for the metric set.

38
00:02:19.777 --> 00:02:23.510
Lab 2 runs in a test
tenant, never production.

39
00:02:27.260 --> 00:02:31.997
Unit 1 is automated investigation
and response, set per device group.

40
00:02:31.997 --> 00:02:34.522
Microsoft Learn lists five levels.

41
00:02:34.522 --> 00:02:37.435
Full, remediate threats automatically.

42
00:02:37.435 --> 00:02:41.290
Three semi levels, requiring
approval for core folders,

43
00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.972
for non-temp folders, or for all folders.

44
00:02:43.972 --> 00:02:45.822
And no automated response.

45
00:02:45.822 --> 00:02:49.073
The program's choices are
risk-based recommendations.

46
00:02:49.073 --> 00:02:53.809
The pilot puts ring one's workstation
group at semi, require approval for all

47
00:02:53.809 --> 00:02:56.666
folders, so every
remediation is proposed and

48
00:02:56.666 --> 00:02:59.373
an analyst approves it
in the Action center.

49
00:02:59.373 --> 00:03:01.568
Those two weeks of pending actions,

50
00:03:01.568 --> 00:03:04.823
with the approval rate
recorded, are the audit phase.

51
00:03:04.823 --> 00:03:08.272
Then workstation groups go to
full, which Learn recommends.

52
00:03:08.272 --> 00:03:10.485
Server groups stay at a semi level,

53
00:03:10.485 --> 00:03:15.147
because isolating a server is never
autonomous in the program's ladder.

54
00:03:15.147 --> 00:03:18.449
Rollback sets a device
group back to a semi level,

55
00:03:18.449 --> 00:03:23.648
on the Devices page under automated
responses in the Defender X D R settings.

56
00:03:23.648 --> 00:03:26.024
Learn says changing device groups needs

57
00:03:26.024 --> 00:03:29.310
Global Administrator or
Security Administrator.

58
00:03:33.060 --> 00:03:36.459
Automatic attack disruption
has its own exclusions,

59
00:03:36.459 --> 00:03:40.012
under the automated response
settings in Defender X D R.

60
00:03:40.012 --> 00:03:44.184
Microsoft Learn says the identities
page holds user exclusions,

61
00:03:44.184 --> 00:03:47.660
and the devices page holds
device groups and I P ranges.

62
00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:51.791
Learn names service accounts and
emergency admin accounts as the use

63
00:03:51.791 --> 00:03:55.922
for user exclusions, and cautions
that exclusions reduce protection.

64
00:03:55.922 --> 00:04:00.758
So the program excludes exactly two
kinds of identity: the emergency access

65
00:04:00.758 --> 00:04:05.823
accounts, and the service accounts whose
disablement would stop the S O C itself.

66
00:04:05.823 --> 00:04:09.473
Learn adds that excluding a device
group from attack disruption

67
00:04:09.473 --> 00:04:13.260
also affects automated investigation
and response for that group.

68
00:04:13.260 --> 00:04:16.640
Each exclusion goes in the
register with the authorizing

69
00:04:16.640 --> 00:04:19.598
official's approval, and
is reviewed every quarter.

70
00:04:19.598 --> 00:04:21.135
Two more facts from Learn.

71
00:04:21.135 --> 00:04:24.716
The contain user action needs
sensor version ten point

72
00:04:24.716 --> 00:04:27.985
eight four seven zero or
later, which the lab checks.

73
00:04:27.985 --> 00:04:31.998
And attack disruption's availability
in G C C isn't specified

74
00:04:31.998 --> 00:04:35.970
on Learn's government pages,
so confirm it in the tenant.

75
00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:41.545
Unit 2 is operations.

76
00:04:41.545 --> 00:04:46.252
The operating rhythm belongs to the
Security Operations and Telemetry book;

77
00:04:46.252 --> 00:04:48.679
this gate produces Defender's part of it,

78
00:04:48.679 --> 00:04:52.283
on a cadence that's the program's
risk-based recommendation.

79
00:04:52.283 --> 00:04:56.709
Weekly: sensor health and coverage, with
a ticket for every device not active,

80
00:04:56.709 --> 00:04:59.730
and prevention and Attack
Surface Reduction drift,

81
00:04:59.730 --> 00:05:03.383
with the policy export compared
against the last approved one.

82
00:05:03.383 --> 00:05:08.414
Quarterly: detection tuning for each
rule, the exception register review,

83
00:05:08.414 --> 00:05:12.044
and an exercise, a tabletop
plus the synthetic events.

84
00:05:12.044 --> 00:05:15.808
Monthly: the metrics and the
evidence package, hashed.

85
00:05:15.808 --> 00:05:19.108
The monthly evidence run is a
short script from the guide.

86
00:05:19.108 --> 00:05:24.101
It runs the read-only coverage, Attack
Surface Reduction and prevention queries,

87
00:05:24.101 --> 00:05:27.381
exports the last thirty days
of incidents with their

88
00:05:27.381 --> 00:05:30.958
classification and determination,
and hashes every file.

89
00:05:30.958 --> 00:05:32.383
Why file the results?

90
00:05:32.383 --> 00:05:36.332
Because Microsoft Learn says
advanced hunting keeps thirty days.

91
00:05:36.332 --> 00:05:40.190
The monthly run is how anyone
can answer for an older month.

92
00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:47.318
The metrics measure protected
coverage and effectiveness,

93
00:05:47.318 --> 00:05:48.940
not whether a setting exists.

94
00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:52.427
They join Security Operations
and Telemetry's metric set,

95
00:05:52.427 --> 00:05:54.752
and the targets are the program's to set.

96
00:05:54.752 --> 00:05:56.791
Four examples from the guide's seven.

97
00:05:56.791 --> 00:06:00.662
Protected coverage is onboarded
devices with an active sensor,

98
00:06:00.662 --> 00:06:04.388
divided by in-scope devices in
the inventory, because a policy

99
00:06:04.388 --> 00:06:08.552
assigned to a group says nothing
about devices that never checked in.

100
00:06:08.552 --> 00:06:13.415
Enforcement depth is devices with every
Attack Surface Reduction rule in block,

101
00:06:13.415 --> 00:06:16.752
or an approved exception,
divided by onboarded devices,

102
00:06:16.752 --> 00:06:20.090
because audit mode is configured
but protects nothing.

103
00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:23.616
Detection validity is enabled
detections with a passing

104
00:06:23.616 --> 00:06:27.215
known-bad test this quarter,
divided by enabled detections.

105
00:06:27.215 --> 00:06:30.488
And synthetic-event success
is events that produced

106
00:06:30.488 --> 00:06:33.165
their expected alert,
divided by events run.

107
00:06:33.165 --> 00:06:37.426
The synthetic events, run on ring
zero devices under a change record,

108
00:06:37.426 --> 00:06:41.002
are the detection test, the
network protection test site,

109
00:06:41.002 --> 00:06:44.351
a controlled folder access
write by an untrusted app,

110
00:06:44.351 --> 00:06:48.840
a demonstration for each rule, and a
custom detection's known-bad replay.

111
00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:53.960
They show an assessor that telemetry,
detection and routing work end to end.

112
00:06:57.710 --> 00:07:02.510
Lab 2 runs the guide's validate steps
in a test tenant, never production.

113
00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:05.510
The authors haven't run
them, as the page says.

114
00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:09.835
Every step reads, apart from the evidence
files it writes in your own folder.

115
00:07:09.835 --> 00:07:13.430
You need Module 1's test tenant,
reader account and test device,

116
00:07:13.430 --> 00:07:17.518
an account that can view the Defender
portal's settings, and the ladder

117
00:07:17.518 --> 00:07:21.747
decision record for the test tenant, or
the guide's Step 9 as the reference.

118
00:07:21.747 --> 00:07:24.097
Step one runs on the test device.

119
00:07:24.097 --> 00:07:28.723
It's Microsoft Learn's command that reads
the sensor version from the registry.

120
00:07:28.723 --> 00:07:32.684
Contain user needs ten point
eight four seven zero or later.

121
00:07:32.684 --> 00:07:36.422
Step two is a click path in the
Defender portal for your cloud.

122
00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:41.686
Open the devices page under automated
responses and note each device group's level.

123
00:07:41.686 --> 00:07:45.485
Then open the identities page
and note the excluded users.

124
00:07:45.485 --> 00:07:47.923
Compare both with the
ladder decision record.

125
00:07:47.923 --> 00:07:51.126
The guide's pass: every
server group at a semi level,

126
00:07:51.126 --> 00:07:54.798
every workstation group at
full or a recorded exception,

127
00:07:54.798 --> 00:07:59.210
and the emergency access accounts
in the identity exclusions.

128
00:08:02.960 --> 00:08:05.987
Step three is the guide's
monthly evidence run,

129
00:08:05.987 --> 00:08:08.935
shortened on the slide;
the page has it in full.

130
00:08:08.935 --> 00:08:14.185
Use a new, empty evidence folder,
because the run hashes every file in it.

131
00:08:14.185 --> 00:08:18.030
It connects, runs the three
read-only queries for coverage,

132
00:08:18.030 --> 00:08:22.435
Attack Surface Reduction and
prevention, and writes each to a file.

133
00:08:22.435 --> 00:08:27.553
It exports the last thirty days of incidents,
filtered on the created date time,

134
00:08:27.553 --> 00:08:30.387
which Microsoft Learn lists as filterable,

135
00:08:30.387 --> 00:08:34.560
with their severity, status,
classification and determination.

136
00:08:34.560 --> 00:08:38.435
Then it hashes every file in
the folder into a checksum file.

137
00:08:38.435 --> 00:08:40.531
You should see the coverage, A S R,

138
00:08:40.531 --> 00:08:44.160
prevention and incidents
files, and the checksum file.

139
00:08:44.160 --> 00:08:48.748
Step four checks the hashes, the way
next month's run checks last month's.

140
00:08:48.748 --> 00:08:52.740
It reads the checksum file, leaves
out the checksum file's own entry,

141
00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.643
since the run writes it
into the folder it hashes,

142
00:08:55.643 --> 00:08:58.547
and compares each recorded
hash with a fresh one.

143
00:08:58.547 --> 00:09:01.373
Matches should be true
for every evidence file.

144
00:09:01.373 --> 00:09:03.997
False means a file changed
after it was filed.

145
00:09:03.997 --> 00:09:08.870
Step five backs it out: nothing
changed in the tenant, so disconnect.

146
00:09:12.620 --> 00:09:15.932
The federal requirement, from
the guide's requirements table.

147
00:09:15.932 --> 00:09:21.693
Gate nine supports I R four, enhancement
one, automated incident handling processes,

148
00:09:21.693 --> 00:09:26.631
and S I four, enhancement seven,
automated response to suspicious events,

149
00:09:26.631 --> 00:09:31.569
from NIST special publication eight
hundred fifty-three, revision five.

150
00:09:31.569 --> 00:09:34.169
The evidence is the device group levels,

151
00:09:34.169 --> 00:09:37.083
the exclusions, and the
Action center export.

152
00:09:37.083 --> 00:09:41.566
Gate ten supports C A seven,
continuous monitoring; A U eleven,

153
00:09:41.566 --> 00:09:46.320
audit record retention; and I R
three, incident response testing.

154
00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:50.095
The evidence is the monthly
package and the exercise record.

155
00:09:50.095 --> 00:09:54.201
Each row lines up with fed-ramp
twenty X key security indicators,

156
00:09:54.201 --> 00:09:59.470
which bind cloud service providers; the
agency lines its own evidence up with them.

157
00:09:59.470 --> 00:10:04.095
The guide counts Gates two, seven
and ten, with the synthetic events,

158
00:10:04.095 --> 00:10:07.207
as the closure map's
audit and visibility row.

159
00:10:07.207 --> 00:10:09.298
The gates support these controls,

160
00:10:09.298 --> 00:10:12.870
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

161
00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:18.595
Check yourself with two questions.

162
00:10:18.595 --> 00:10:22.570
First: what level does the program
set for a server device group?

163
00:10:22.570 --> 00:10:24.995
The answer is b, a semi level.

164
00:10:24.995 --> 00:10:29.530
Microsoft recommends full, and the
program uses full for workstations,

165
00:10:29.530 --> 00:10:33.757
but isolating a server is never
autonomous in the program's ladder.

166
00:10:33.757 --> 00:10:38.633
Second: an assessor asks for the coverage
query result from four months ago.

167
00:10:38.633 --> 00:10:39.807
Where does it come from?

168
00:10:39.807 --> 00:10:42.758
The answer is b: that
month's evidence run.

169
00:10:42.758 --> 00:10:47.725
Advanced hunting keeps thirty days, so
the filed and hashed result is the record,

170
00:10:47.725 --> 00:10:50.208
and its checksum shows it hasn't changed.

171
00:10:50.208 --> 00:10:55.370
The module page has two more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

172
00:10:59.120 --> 00:10:59.945
To sum up.

173
00:10:59.945 --> 00:11:02.945
You checked the sensor version
that contain user needs.

174
00:11:02.945 --> 00:11:05.609
You compared the device
group levels and the attack

175
00:11:05.609 --> 00:11:08.520
disruption exclusions with
the ladder decision record.

176
00:11:08.520 --> 00:11:10.484
You ran the monthly evidence run,

177
00:11:10.484 --> 00:11:13.757
and you checked its hashes
the way next month's run will.

178
00:11:13.757 --> 00:11:16.443
The evidence an I S S O
keeps from this module:

179
00:11:16.443 --> 00:11:19.577
the device group list with
each remediation level,

180
00:11:19.577 --> 00:11:24.724
the attack disruption exclusions with
their approvals, the monthly Action center

181
00:11:24.724 --> 00:11:27.111
export, the quarterly exercise record,

182
00:11:27.111 --> 00:11:30.020
and the monthly package
with its checksum file.

183
00:11:30.020 --> 00:11:31.895
That completes the learning path.

184
00:11:31.895 --> 00:11:36.404
From here, work the implementation
guide's checklist, gate by gate,

185
00:11:36.404 --> 00:11:39.995
from one to ten, and keep
the evidence each gate names.

186
00:11:39.995 --> 00:11:44.480
Thank you for following Defender
X D R Engineering and Operations.
